---
// The web-UI section: what server mode is, what it looks like, and the answer to
// the question every developer asks straight after "drive your agents from a
// browser", which is "hosted where, reachable by whom, and is there a login".
//
// SCREENSHOTS ARE OPTIONAL AND REAL.
// This section is built to receive two genuine captures. Neither is in the
// repository yet, and nothing here fakes one: `publicPng` returns null when a
// file is absent and the figure is skipped, so the section renders complete
// without them and gains the images the moment they land in `public/`.
//
//   public/dux-web-desktop.png  a landscape capture of the web UI on a desktop
//                               browser, sidebar + terminal pane + changes pane
//                               visible. Target 2560x1600 (2x of 1280x800).
//                               Any 16:10-ish landscape size works; the markup
//                               reads the real dimensions off the file.
//
//   public/dux-web-phone.png    a PORTRAIT capture of the mobile shell on a
//                               phone, ideally showing an agent's terminal with
//                               the accessory bar and compose bar. Target
//                               1170x2532 (iPhone native).
//
// PNG only (the size is read from the PNG header). `npm run prep` writes a
// `.webp` sibling for each automatically, and this picks it up when present.
import { publicPng } from "../lib/public-image";

const desktop = publicPng("dux-web-desktop");
const phone = publicPng("dux-web-phone");

// The startup banner, reproduced from the real one so it is not invented:
// `dux-web`'s banner renderer prints one indented `➜ <label>: <url>` row per
// bound listener (crates/dux-web/src/console.rs), and the labels are
// "Local (loopback)" for the loopback leg and "Tailscale" for the best-effort
// tailnet leg (crates/dux-web/src/lib.rs). 100.64.0.5 stands in for a tailnet
// address; Tailscale hands those out of 100.64.0.0/10.
const bannerRows = [
  { label: "Local (loopback)", url: "http://127.0.0.1:8080" },
  { label: "Tailscale", url: "http://100.64.0.5:8080" },
];
---

<section id="web" class="anchor-offset py-16 sm:py-24">
  <div class="container-app">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <span class="pill">▸ remote work</span>
      <h2 class="heading mt-3 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        Same office. <span class="text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">Any window.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        dux has two front ends over one engine: a terminal UI and a web UI. Both are
        first class, and both are staying. They share the same projects, the same
        agents, the same worktrees and the same config file, so an agent you start
        in one is the same agent in the other.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        One dux owns your dux directory at a time, so moving between the two is a
        hand-off rather than a second window: a palette command hands the running
        engine straight to the web server, and leaving that screen drops you back
        into the terminal around the same still-running engine. Nothing relaunches
        and no conversation is lost. Browsers, though, are plural. Point as many at
        the workspace as you like and they all see the same session at the same
        moment.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        You will not find a feature-by-feature table of the two here, because a
        table like that is stale the week after it is written. Each surface tells
        you what it can do: in the terminal, the help overlay and the command
        palette; in the browser, the cog menu and the row menus.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      (desktop || phone) && (
        <div class="mt-10 grid items-start gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[1.55fr_0.45fr]">
          {desktop && (
            <figure class="min-w-0">
              <div
                class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)]"
                style="box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-pink) 8%, transparent), 0 30px 80px -30px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-pink) 26%, transparent);"
              >
                <picture>
                  {desktop.webp && <source srcset={desktop.webp} type="image/webp" />}
                  <img
                    src={desktop.src}
                    alt="The dux web UI in a desktop browser: a sidebar of projects, agent sessions and terminals, an agent's live terminal in the center, and the changed-files pane on the right."
                    width={desktop.width}
                    height={desktop.height}
                    loading="lazy"
                    class="block w-full bg-[color:var(--color-ink)]"
                  />
                </picture>
              </div>
              <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">
                the browser · same workspace · same live PTYs
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          )}
          {phone && (
            <figure class="mx-auto min-w-0 max-w-[280px] lg:max-w-none">
              <div
                class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)]"
                style="box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-accent) 8%, transparent), 0 30px 80px -30px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-accent) 26%, transparent);"
              >
                <picture>
                  {phone.webp && <source srcset={phone.webp} type="image/webp" />}
                  <img
                    src={phone.src}
                    alt="The dux web UI on a phone in portrait: an agent's terminal filling the screen with the accessory key bar and compose bar below it."
                    width={phone.width}
                    height={phone.height}
                    loading="lazy"
                    class="block w-full bg-[color:var(--color-ink)]"
                  />
                </picture>
              </div>
              <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">
                and the couch
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          )}
        </div>
      )
    }

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[0.95fr_1.05fr] lg:gap-10">
      <div class="term relative self-start">
        <div class="term-bar">
          <span class="term-dot" style="background:#ff5f56"></span>
          <span class="term-dot" style="background:#febc2e"></span>
          <span class="term-dot" style="background:#28c840"></span>
          <span class="ml-3 mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">what you type</span>
        </div>
        {/* One element per line rather than a <pre>: inside a <pre> every space
            in this file's own indentation would be significant and land in the
            rendered output. Here whitespace collapses, the indent is padding,
            and each row scrolls instead of wrapping. */}
        <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-[color:var(--color-ink)] p-4 sm:p-5">
          <div class="mono w-max text-[12px] leading-relaxed sm:text-[13px]">
            <div class="whitespace-nowrap">
              <span class="text-[color:var(--color-dim)]">$</span>{" "}
              <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux server</span>
            </div>
            {
              bannerRows.map((row) => (
                <div class="whitespace-nowrap pl-3">
                  <span class="text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">➜</span>{" "}
                  <span class="text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">{row.label}:</span>{" "}
                  <span class="text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">{row.url}</span>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="heading text-xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-2xl">
          Where it runs, and who can reach it
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
          It runs on your machine. dux serves the web UI itself, out of the same
          binary, and there is no cloud in the middle of it.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)]">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">✦</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Loopback by default.</strong>
              <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">dux server</span> binds
              <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">127.0.0.1:8080</span>, so
              out of the box nothing leaves the machine.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">✦</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Your tailnet, if you have one.</strong>
              When the <span class="mono text-[color:var(--color-text)]">tailscale</span> CLI
              is around, dux also binds this machine's Tailscale address on the same
              port, so your own devices reach it over WireGuard. That leg is opt-out.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">✦</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">There is no login.</strong>
              No password, no token, no accounts. dux is single-tenant on purpose and
              server mode says so rather than pretending otherwise.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-pink)]">✦</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">So everyone who can reach it shares one workspace.</strong>
              They can drive any agent or terminal, browse the server's filesystem,
              and edit files in your worktrees. Access control is entirely a question
              of where you bind.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-[color:var(--color-warning)]">⚠</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Bind wider and dux says so, loudly.</strong>
              Point it at a LAN or public address and the banner tells you it is
              reachable with no login and that anyone who gets there controls your
              agents and worktrees. Loopback, your tailnet, or a reverse proxy you
              authenticate yourself are the safe shapes.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <a
          href="/docs/server-mode"
          class="mt-5 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-muted)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-accent)]"
        >
          the whole story, flags and every <span class="mono">[server]</span> key
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
